1
00:02:20,774 --> 00:02:22,786
Oh, Kevin.

2
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:24,387
Hi, Clark.

3
00:02:24,411 --> 00:02:26,156
What happened to you?

4
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,124
You were due back yesterday.

5
00:02:28,148 --> 00:02:29,859
Well, I got hung
up there overnight

6
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:32,317
with Mark and a couple
of his programmers.

7
00:02:33,954 --> 00:02:35,287
So how did you do?

8
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:37,934
They like their program.

9
00:02:37,958 --> 00:02:40,136
Oh, great.

10
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,772
With a couple of modifications,
I think they'll go for it.

11
00:02:42,796 --> 00:02:44,074
Kevin, we've gotta talk.

12
00:02:44,098 --> 00:02:46,042
Look, I'm up to my
neck in paperwork.

13
00:02:46,066 --> 00:02:47,510
Why don't you fill
me in over dinner?

14
00:02:47,534 --> 00:02:49,078
I can't make it tonight.

15
00:02:49,102 --> 00:02:51,648
I promised Diane we'd
have dinner together at home.

16
00:02:51,672 --> 00:02:53,617
Kevin, I've gotta know
what kind of modifications

17
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:55,218
we're talking about here.

18
00:02:55,242 --> 00:02:57,754
Clark, I haven't seen
her in over a week.

19
00:02:57,778 --> 00:02:59,956
She's my wife. I'd
like to keep it that way.

20
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:02,625
Kevin... Let's have breakfast.

21
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:04,427
You know I don't eat breakfast.

22
00:03:04,451 --> 00:03:05,562
That's right,

23
00:03:05,586 --> 00:03:07,063
and you remember
our mother warned you

24
00:03:07,087 --> 00:03:08,298
that if you didn't,

25
00:03:08,322 --> 00:03:10,600
you wouldn't grow up
big and strong like me.

26
00:03:19,899 --> 00:03:20,977
Hello?

27
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:22,678
Hi, sweetheart.

28
00:03:22,702 --> 00:03:23,980
I'm back.

29
00:03:24,004 --> 00:03:25,181
Hi.

30
00:03:25,205 --> 00:03:26,549
How was your trip?

31
00:03:26,573 --> 00:03:28,517
<i>Great. It was great.</i>

32
00:03:28,541 --> 00:03:30,387
But there's a couple of
things I've gotta go over

33
00:03:30,411 --> 00:03:31,922
with Clark right away,

34
00:03:31,946 --> 00:03:34,557
and I'm afraid I won't be able
to make it home for dinner.

35
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,226
<i>Oh, no.</i>

36
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:37,994
<i>I've got everything
started already.</i>

37
00:03:38,018 --> 00:03:40,063
Well, uh,

38
00:03:40,087 --> 00:03:43,066
it'll keep until
tomorrow night, won't it?

39
00:03:43,090 --> 00:03:44,967
Not really.

40
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:48,438
<i>Well, I'm sorry,
darling. Believe me.</i>

41
00:03:48,462 --> 00:03:51,407
The reason I'm
working so hard is for us.

42
00:03:51,431 --> 00:03:53,175
You know that, don't you?

43
00:03:53,199 --> 00:03:56,346
I just wanted you to come
home to something special.

44
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:59,482
I am. You.

45
00:03:59,506 --> 00:04:01,484
I love you.

46
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:03,319
I love you too.

47
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:21,971
Hello?

48
00:04:21,995 --> 00:04:24,073
Yes, it is.

49
00:04:24,097 --> 00:04:25,664
Yes, he's with his brother.

50
00:04:30,236 --> 00:04:31,648
That's impossible.

51
00:04:31,672 --> 00:04:33,215
Who is this?

52
00:04:33,239 --> 00:04:36,786
Hello? Hello?

53
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:39,656
Here, kitty, kitty, kitty.

54
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:40,990
Here, kitty, kitty, kitty.

55
00:05:31,131 --> 00:05:32,463
Kevin?

56
00:05:36,136 --> 00:05:38,281
Diane.

57
00:05:38,305 --> 00:05:39,949
What are you doing here?

58
00:05:39,973 --> 00:05:41,550
Someone called me
and said you'd be here.

59
00:05:41,574 --> 00:05:42,719
Some woman.

60
00:05:42,743 --> 00:05:45,254
Really, Kevin, where
are your manners?

61
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:46,990
Introduce us.

62
00:05:47,014 --> 00:05:49,358
Let's just go outside...
Kevin, what's going on?

63
00:05:49,382 --> 00:05:52,562
I'm Elizabeth
Benson. And you're...?

64
00:05:52,586 --> 00:05:54,831
Diane Benson.

65
00:05:54,855 --> 00:05:57,500
What a coincidence.

66
00:05:57,524 --> 00:05:59,569
We all have the same last name.

67
00:05:59,593 --> 00:06:01,905
Perhaps my husband
could explain.

68
00:06:01,929 --> 00:06:03,394
Darling?

69
00:06:04,364 --> 00:06:05,608
Your husband?

70
00:06:05,632 --> 00:06:06,976
He's my husband.

71
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,678
Yours too.

72
00:06:08,702 --> 00:06:10,312
Really, Kevin.

73
00:06:10,336 --> 00:06:13,015
An extramarital
fling is one thing,

74
00:06:13,039 --> 00:06:15,518
but an entire second wife?

75
00:06:23,583 --> 00:06:28,031
Well, one lousy turn
deserves another.

76
00:06:28,055 --> 00:06:29,132
You did that.

77
00:06:29,156 --> 00:06:32,190
Yes, I suppose I did.

78
00:06:35,996 --> 00:06:37,429
Diane?

79
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:40,064
Diane?

80
00:06:42,702 --> 00:06:44,736
Diane, where are you?

81
00:06:46,873 --> 00:06:48,218
Oh. No, Kevin.

82
00:06:48,242 --> 00:06:50,019
Stop. No, you've gotta
let me explain to you.

83
00:06:50,043 --> 00:06:51,687
Get out of my way. I...

84
00:06:51,711 --> 00:06:53,356
I don't love her. I love you.

85
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:54,824
Kevin, I... Let
go of me, please.

86
00:06:54,848 --> 00:06:55,992
No, no. Please, Kevin.

87
00:06:56,016 --> 00:06:58,094
I'm not gonna lose you, damn it.

88
00:06:58,118 --> 00:07:00,163
Don't.

89
00:07:00,187 --> 00:07:02,053
Stop!

90
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:07,503
Diane.

91
00:07:30,417 --> 00:07:32,294
Who is it?

92
00:07:32,318 --> 00:07:33,752
Diane.

93
00:07:39,592 --> 00:07:41,971
Are you okay?

94
00:07:41,995 --> 00:07:43,639
Not really.

95
00:07:43,663 --> 00:07:45,975
Oh, oh, it's gonna be all right.

96
00:07:45,999 --> 00:07:47,744
Come on in.

97
00:07:47,768 --> 00:07:50,301
It's gonna be okay.
Just come over here...

98
00:07:54,974 --> 00:07:56,985
Oh, you don't have to do that.

99
00:07:57,009 --> 00:07:59,021
Oh, I've gotta do something.

100
00:07:59,045 --> 00:08:00,757
How do you feel?

101
00:08:00,781 --> 00:08:02,658
I don't know.

102
00:08:02,682 --> 00:08:04,527
I'm gonna go home.

103
00:08:04,551 --> 00:08:06,328
Do you think that's a good idea?

104
00:08:06,352 --> 00:08:10,132
Well, I... I gotta try
to understand this.

105
00:08:10,156 --> 00:08:12,401
Do you want me to come
along for moral support?

106
00:08:12,425 --> 00:08:15,771
That's okay. Let me
follow you in my car.

107
00:08:33,813 --> 00:08:36,459
I live here. I live here.

108
00:08:36,483 --> 00:08:38,428
He said we'd have to
have breakfast together

109
00:08:38,452 --> 00:08:40,985
because he was having
dinner with his wife.

110
00:08:42,221 --> 00:08:43,499
I live here. Let me through.

111
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:45,883
What happened? Excuse me.

112
00:08:48,128 --> 00:08:49,505
Mrs. Benson? Yes.

113
00:08:49,529 --> 00:08:51,440
Lieutenant Herrmann,
Atlanta Police.

114
00:08:51,464 --> 00:08:53,175
What's going on,
lieutenant? What happened?

115
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:55,177
I'm afraid your husband's dead.

116
00:08:55,201 --> 00:08:58,280
It appears that someone
hit him with that vase,

117
00:08:58,304 --> 00:08:59,915
causing him to fall
and fracture his skull

118
00:08:59,939 --> 00:09:01,751
on the corner of that table.

119
00:09:01,775 --> 00:09:05,087
No. He was alive
when I left him.

120
00:09:05,111 --> 00:09:07,490
We struggled, I-I guess
I hit him with that vase,

121
00:09:07,514 --> 00:09:10,926
but he fell right there
and he was alive.

122
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:12,195
You did what?

123
00:09:12,219 --> 00:09:14,397
Maybe we'd better talk
about this downtown.

124
00:09:14,421 --> 00:09:17,199
Uh, first she's going
to talk to her lawyer.

125
00:09:23,196 --> 00:09:25,363
Hi. Sorry I'm late.

126
00:09:26,733 --> 00:09:28,010
What are you doing?

127
00:09:28,034 --> 00:09:30,245
I'm trying to type this brief.

128
00:09:30,269 --> 00:09:32,982
It has to be filed by noon.

129
00:09:33,006 --> 00:09:34,750
Typing's not one
of my best things.

130
00:09:34,774 --> 00:09:37,253
This correction fluid runs.

131
00:09:37,277 --> 00:09:39,043
Oh, I'll do it.

132
00:09:40,347 --> 00:09:42,391
I'll have it typed
and filed in an hour.

133
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:44,560
Okay.

134
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:46,128
Where were you
this morning, anyway?

135
00:09:46,152 --> 00:09:48,063
Oh, my car.

136
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:50,266
Some hose came
loose under the hood

137
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:52,301
and squirted oil everywhere.

138
00:09:52,325 --> 00:09:53,902
I had to be towed.

139
00:09:53,926 --> 00:09:56,706
I had to go home
and change my suit.

140
00:09:56,730 --> 00:09:58,775
Well, I was trying
to get ahold of you.

141
00:09:58,799 --> 00:10:00,643
What'd you want?

142
00:10:00,667 --> 00:10:02,611
A friend of mine, Diane Jonas...

143
00:10:02,635 --> 00:10:04,180
Or Diane Benson,
she just got married.

144
00:10:04,204 --> 00:10:07,538
The police have charged her
with murdering her husband.

145
00:10:08,742 --> 00:10:10,886
I finally got ahold of Michelle.

146
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:13,656
She got her out? Yeah.

147
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:15,813
Her husband was a bigamist.

148
00:10:17,050 --> 00:10:19,295
A bigamist?

149
00:10:19,319 --> 00:10:22,198
Mm. Neither of the wives
knew anything about each other.

150
00:10:22,222 --> 00:10:25,735
How long had this friend of
yours been married to him?

151
00:10:25,759 --> 00:10:27,370
Five months.

152
00:10:27,394 --> 00:10:29,739
How long had he been
married to the other one?

153
00:10:29,763 --> 00:10:31,173
Don't know.

154
00:10:31,197 --> 00:10:32,808
I'll tell you what,

155
00:10:32,832 --> 00:10:34,343
when you finish
typing that brief,

156
00:10:34,367 --> 00:10:36,379
I'll take it over to
the courthouse.

157
00:10:36,403 --> 00:10:38,014
Oh, okay.

158
00:10:38,038 --> 00:10:40,649
Mark Herrmann had
a birthday last week.

159
00:10:40,673 --> 00:10:41,850
Oh, yeah? Yeah.

160
00:10:41,874 --> 00:10:44,434
I want to ask him how
he feels about that.

161
00:10:45,679 --> 00:10:47,390
A bigamist, huh?

162
00:10:55,388 --> 00:10:56,999
Hi, Ben.

163
00:10:57,023 --> 00:10:59,135
Oh, hi, Michelle.

164
00:10:59,159 --> 00:11:01,003
Cassie says you
signed up a friend.

165
00:11:01,027 --> 00:11:02,171
Yeah. What's her name?

166
00:11:02,195 --> 00:11:03,438
Diane Benson. Yeah.

167
00:11:03,462 --> 00:11:05,241
It's not gonna be easy.

168
00:11:05,265 --> 00:11:08,277
Her fingerprints were all over
the vase she struck him with.

169
00:11:08,301 --> 00:11:10,012
They had a loud,
public argument.

170
00:11:10,036 --> 00:11:13,215
He was two-timing
her in a big way.

171
00:11:13,239 --> 00:11:15,651
And the next-door neighbor said
that Diane was the only one seen

172
00:11:15,675 --> 00:11:17,319
entering and leaving
the house that night.

173
00:11:17,343 --> 00:11:18,620
Yeah. That's what Mark says.

174
00:11:18,644 --> 00:11:20,256
Who? I was in talking

175
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:22,024
with Mark Herrmann
about his birthday.

176
00:11:22,048 --> 00:11:23,080
Oh...

177
00:11:25,819 --> 00:11:29,799
I wonder how a fellow keeps
something like that a secret.

178
00:11:29,823 --> 00:11:30,900
A second wife? Yeah.

179
00:11:30,924 --> 00:11:32,969
Beats me.

180
00:11:32,993 --> 00:11:36,471
I guess there were
times he had to look busy.

181
00:11:37,897 --> 00:11:39,330
Yeah.

182
00:11:41,134 --> 00:11:42,645
I really wonder how
he did it, though.

183
00:11:42,669 --> 00:11:44,480
I mean, how'd he keep
everything straight?

184
00:11:44,504 --> 00:11:46,149
You know, phone calls, bills,

185
00:11:46,173 --> 00:11:47,850
and which one liked roses,

186
00:11:47,874 --> 00:11:49,152
which one liked daffodils?

187
00:11:49,176 --> 00:11:51,354
Which one meat, which one fish?

188
00:11:51,378 --> 00:11:53,356
Hell, how did he keep
their names straight?

189
00:11:54,947 --> 00:11:57,527
I don't know, but I
believe she's innocent.

190
00:11:57,551 --> 00:11:59,028
Well, that's a start.

191
00:11:59,052 --> 00:12:01,697
You know, sometimes
it bothers me,

192
00:12:01,721 --> 00:12:04,600
living alone with no woman.

193
00:12:04,624 --> 00:12:07,104
But I'm damned if I'm
gonna live with two of them.

194
00:12:24,477 --> 00:12:26,955
Oh, dear.

195
00:12:26,979 --> 00:12:29,692
Are you all right?

196
00:12:29,716 --> 00:12:31,794
Is this Kevin's briefcase?

197
00:12:31,818 --> 00:12:34,296
Yes.

198
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:37,500
Then why didn't
the police take it?

199
00:12:37,524 --> 00:12:39,535
I don't know.

200
00:12:39,559 --> 00:12:41,804
Do you know the combination?

201
00:12:41,828 --> 00:12:44,695
Nine, one, three. His birthday.

202
00:12:46,132 --> 00:12:49,578
Your husband went on these
out of town trips each week?

203
00:12:49,602 --> 00:12:51,213
Yes.

204
00:12:51,237 --> 00:12:54,016
Did he leave you a number
where he could be reached?

205
00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:56,852
No, uh, he always called
me at 8 in the morning

206
00:12:56,876 --> 00:13:00,522
or 8 in the evening,
always without fail.

207
00:13:00,546 --> 00:13:04,326
He never told you
where he was staying?

208
00:13:04,350 --> 00:13:06,762
No, and I-I never asked.

209
00:13:06,786 --> 00:13:09,498
When he was home,
did you go out very often?

210
00:13:09,522 --> 00:13:11,789
No. We hardly went out at all.

211
00:13:25,038 --> 00:13:26,949
Diane, what did...?
He used to say

212
00:13:26,973 --> 00:13:28,183
there's nothing he liked better

213
00:13:28,207 --> 00:13:30,842
than coming home
to a meal I cooked.

214
00:13:32,211 --> 00:13:35,290
He seemed so
happy with our life.

215
00:13:40,786 --> 00:13:42,398
Mm.

216
00:13:42,422 --> 00:13:43,632
How'd it go today?

217
00:13:43,656 --> 00:13:46,668
All I could think about
was coming home.

218
00:13:46,692 --> 00:13:48,037
I love you.

219
00:13:48,061 --> 00:13:50,139
I made a pot roast.

220
00:13:50,163 --> 00:13:51,374
I love your pot roast.

221
00:14:09,815 --> 00:14:11,315
Excuse me.

222
00:14:22,562 --> 00:14:24,306
It's gonna take some time.

223
00:14:24,330 --> 00:14:25,629
Yeah.

224
00:14:27,667 --> 00:14:30,579
Well, there's nothing
interesting in the briefcase.

225
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:36,285
Hi. Hi.

226
00:14:36,309 --> 00:14:38,220
Hi. MATLOCK: Hi.

227
00:14:38,244 --> 00:14:40,990
That her? CASSIE: Yeah.

228
00:14:41,014 --> 00:14:43,159
Pretty.

229
00:14:43,183 --> 00:14:44,593
You don't think she did it.

230
00:14:44,617 --> 00:14:47,096
I really don't.

231
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:48,731
Have you talked, uh,

232
00:14:48,755 --> 00:14:50,266
with the other Mrs. Benson?

233
00:14:50,290 --> 00:14:51,433
What was it, the first one?

234
00:14:51,457 --> 00:14:52,768
Not yet.

235
00:14:52,792 --> 00:14:55,804
I was thinking, I don't
suppose you could...?

236
00:14:55,828 --> 00:15:01,343
Well, uh, I've got
that Jenkins matter.

237
00:15:01,367 --> 00:15:03,412
You could get a continuance.

238
00:15:03,436 --> 00:15:06,849
Oh, well, we all work
out of the same office.

239
00:15:06,873 --> 00:15:08,818
You both do favors
for me all the time.

240
00:15:08,842 --> 00:15:09,985
It's a turnabout.

241
00:15:10,009 --> 00:15:11,153
Fair play. Right. Thanks.

242
00:15:11,177 --> 00:15:12,821
Yeah.

243
00:15:12,845 --> 00:15:17,493
Where is the other,
um, uh, Mrs. Benson?

244
00:15:17,517 --> 00:15:20,863
Um, she's staying downtown
at the Alexander Hotel,

245
00:15:20,887 --> 00:15:23,465
and I'm going over
to Kevin's office.

246
00:15:23,489 --> 00:15:25,334
I'll see you later.
Yeah, uh, me too.

247
00:15:25,358 --> 00:15:29,393
I'll see you later.
So... So long. Ahem.

248
00:15:31,865 --> 00:15:33,843
Two of 'em, huh?

249
00:15:33,867 --> 00:15:36,934
H-How do you suppose he...?

250
00:15:41,874 --> 00:15:44,920
I'm gonna go talk
to your neighbor.

251
00:15:44,944 --> 00:15:47,056
You be okay for a few minutes?

252
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:48,924
Mm-hm.

253
00:15:48,948 --> 00:15:51,093
You're not allergic
to cats, are you?

254
00:15:51,117 --> 00:15:52,261
No. Why?

255
00:15:52,285 --> 00:15:53,596
You'd think cats were

256
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:54,963
disposable bottles or something,

257
00:15:54,987 --> 00:15:56,599
the way people toss
them out in the street.

258
00:15:56,623 --> 00:15:58,066
It's criminal.
It's just criminal.

259
00:15:58,090 --> 00:15:59,401
They should be shot.

260
00:15:59,425 --> 00:16:01,337
The people, not the cats.

261
00:16:01,361 --> 00:16:02,860
Sit down, please.

262
00:16:08,334 --> 00:16:11,446
Mrs. Berino, um,
you told the police

263
00:16:11,470 --> 00:16:13,849
that Diane Benson
was the only person

264
00:16:13,873 --> 00:16:17,319
other than her husband who
entered their home last night.

265
00:16:17,343 --> 00:16:19,221
That's right.

266
00:16:19,245 --> 00:16:21,523
Well, no offense,

267
00:16:21,547 --> 00:16:24,426
but do you always watch what
goes on at your neighbor's home?

268
00:16:24,450 --> 00:16:26,862
I watch TV.

269
00:16:26,886 --> 00:16:28,897
At least I try to.

270
00:16:28,921 --> 00:16:32,067
Frankly, what goes
on outside my window

271
00:16:32,091 --> 00:16:34,570
is much more interesting.

272
00:16:34,594 --> 00:16:35,771
The police have established

273
00:16:35,795 --> 00:16:39,041
that Mr. Benson was
murdered at around 9:30.

274
00:16:39,065 --> 00:16:40,642
Now, Mrs. Berino,

275
00:16:40,666 --> 00:16:43,111
isn't it possible that you
stepped out of the room,

276
00:16:43,135 --> 00:16:46,181
or maybe you dozed
off around that time?

277
00:16:46,205 --> 00:16:47,850
Absolutely not.

278
00:16:47,874 --> 00:16:51,520
I was sitting right here in
this chair petting Louise,

279
00:16:51,544 --> 00:16:55,524
sitting in that chair
the whole time.

280
00:16:55,548 --> 00:16:57,908
At least I think it was Louise.

281
00:17:04,423 --> 00:17:07,069
M-Mrs. Benson? I'm Ben Matlock.

282
00:17:07,093 --> 00:17:09,071
I work with Michelle Thomas.

283
00:17:09,095 --> 00:17:11,840
She's the attorney
for Diane Benson.

284
00:17:11,864 --> 00:17:13,108
Hm. Could I, uh...?

285
00:17:13,132 --> 00:17:14,243
Certainly.

286
00:17:14,267 --> 00:17:15,944
Thank you.

287
00:17:22,441 --> 00:17:26,855
Oh, packing, huh?

288
00:17:26,879 --> 00:17:28,490
I hate funerals.

289
00:17:28,514 --> 00:17:30,959
Never know what to
say to the grieving widow,

290
00:17:30,983 --> 00:17:32,761
especially if I'm one of them.

291
00:17:32,785 --> 00:17:34,262
Where's home?

292
00:17:34,286 --> 00:17:36,398
Savannah.

293
00:17:36,422 --> 00:17:38,100
That's where you
and Kevin lived?

294
00:17:38,124 --> 00:17:40,236
When he was around.

295
00:17:40,260 --> 00:17:42,471
I understand there was
nothing he liked better

296
00:17:42,495 --> 00:17:44,340
than to come back from a trip

297
00:17:44,364 --> 00:17:46,575
to a good home-cooked meal.

298
00:17:46,599 --> 00:17:49,979
I wouldn't know about that.

299
00:18:17,263 --> 00:18:18,807
Liz?

300
00:18:18,831 --> 00:18:21,343
Darling, how was your trip?

301
00:18:21,367 --> 00:18:23,634
All I could think about
was coming home.

302
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:47,135
Did you know that

303
00:18:47,159 --> 00:18:50,205
Kevin had an
office here in Atlanta

304
00:18:50,229 --> 00:18:52,340
and was in business
with his brother?

305
00:18:52,364 --> 00:18:53,675
As far as I knew,

306
00:18:53,699 --> 00:18:56,545
Kevin was a one-man
management consultant firm,

307
00:18:56,569 --> 00:18:58,514
which he ran out of our home.

308
00:18:58,538 --> 00:19:01,283
Kevin bought your
home in Savannah?

309
00:19:01,307 --> 00:19:02,851
About a year after
we were married.

310
00:19:02,875 --> 00:19:07,389
How could he maintain
two separate lifestyles?

311
00:19:08,848 --> 00:19:11,426
Kevin was good
at handling money.

312
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:13,862
Kevin was good
at a lot of things...

313
00:19:13,886 --> 00:19:18,767
Well... not the least
of which was lying.

314
00:19:18,791 --> 00:19:22,905
When did you find out
about the other wife?

315
00:19:22,929 --> 00:19:24,372
Three days ago.

316
00:19:24,396 --> 00:19:27,876
I pulled a muscle at my
evening aerobics class,

317
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:29,945
so I came home early.

318
00:19:29,969 --> 00:19:32,448
That's when I overheard
Kevin on the phone.

319
00:19:32,472 --> 00:19:35,451
So I did some checking
and flew here to Atlanta

320
00:19:35,475 --> 00:19:38,653
for our little surprise
party, you might say.

321
00:19:38,677 --> 00:19:43,125
Oh, you made that
anonymous call to Diane.

322
00:19:43,149 --> 00:19:44,360
Of course.

323
00:19:44,384 --> 00:19:46,094
It wouldn't have been
a party without her.

324
00:19:46,118 --> 00:19:48,997
It must've been quite a scene.

325
00:19:49,021 --> 00:19:50,999
That was the idea.

326
00:19:51,023 --> 00:19:54,836
Of course, uh, another
way of looking at it

327
00:19:54,860 --> 00:19:56,204
was, uh, trying
to point the finger

328
00:19:56,228 --> 00:19:59,474
or put the, you know,
spotlight on Diane

329
00:19:59,498 --> 00:20:02,644
so that the, uh, killer

330
00:20:02,668 --> 00:20:05,001
could kind of slink out of town.

331
00:20:11,244 --> 00:20:14,056
You think I killed Kevin?

332
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:17,559
Can you prove where
you were at 9:30 last night?

333
00:20:17,583 --> 00:20:20,462
No.

334
00:20:20,486 --> 00:20:22,931
But then again, I
don't have to, do I?

335
00:20:22,955 --> 00:20:26,034
Mrs. Benson, you've been, um,

336
00:20:26,058 --> 00:20:27,736
very cooperative.

337
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:29,771
You never...? No.

338
00:20:29,795 --> 00:20:31,773
Never suspected.

339
00:20:31,797 --> 00:20:33,709
I was satisfied.

340
00:20:35,801 --> 00:20:37,246
As I said,

341
00:20:37,270 --> 00:20:40,704
Kevin was good
at a lot of things.

342
00:20:42,675 --> 00:20:43,941
All right.

343
00:20:46,312 --> 00:20:47,823
May I help you?

344
00:20:47,847 --> 00:20:49,992
Oh, hi. You must
be Clark Benson.

345
00:20:50,016 --> 00:20:51,126
I'm Michelle Thomas.

346
00:20:51,150 --> 00:20:52,861
I'm representing
your sister-in-law.

347
00:20:52,885 --> 00:20:55,564
Your secretary told me I
could wait for you in here.

348
00:20:55,588 --> 00:20:57,566
I see you're clearing
out Kevin's office.

349
00:20:57,590 --> 00:20:58,867
I have to.

350
00:20:58,891 --> 00:21:01,536
Without him, I can't
afford this space.

351
00:21:01,560 --> 00:21:03,272
Hell, without him,

352
00:21:03,296 --> 00:21:05,440
I may not be able
to keep the business.

353
00:21:05,464 --> 00:21:07,109
Your brother was very good.

354
00:21:07,133 --> 00:21:10,845
No. My brother was a genius.

355
00:21:10,869 --> 00:21:14,015
See, he was the point man,
always sniffing out new clients.

356
00:21:14,039 --> 00:21:16,818
Me, well, I stayed
behind, got the work done.

357
00:21:16,842 --> 00:21:18,787
If I fell behind, he'd pitch in.

358
00:21:18,811 --> 00:21:20,456
But the minute we got
our heads above water,

359
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:21,857
he'd go out and: bang!

360
00:21:21,881 --> 00:21:23,024
Three new clients.

361
00:21:23,048 --> 00:21:25,394
Sounds profitable.
We did all right.

362
00:21:25,418 --> 00:21:28,530
Any man with two wives has
to be doing better than all right.

363
00:21:28,554 --> 00:21:32,201
Let's just say we had
several very good years.

364
00:21:32,225 --> 00:21:33,635
I loved my brother,

365
00:21:33,659 --> 00:21:35,871
but I sure didn't know him
as well as I thought I did.

366
00:21:35,895 --> 00:21:38,006
You didn't know
he had two wives?

367
00:21:38,030 --> 00:21:41,142
No.

368
00:21:41,166 --> 00:21:44,145
I can't excuse or forgive
Diane for what happened,

369
00:21:44,169 --> 00:21:46,248
but I think I can
understand how she felt.

370
00:21:46,272 --> 00:21:47,750
So, please, just tell her for me

371
00:21:47,774 --> 00:21:49,117
that I'll send her
Kevin's things,

372
00:21:49,141 --> 00:21:51,119
but I don't want
her coming here.

373
00:21:51,143 --> 00:21:53,388
I never wanna see her again.

374
00:21:53,412 --> 00:21:55,257
That may be difficult.

375
00:21:55,281 --> 00:21:57,292
We may need you in court.

376
00:21:57,316 --> 00:21:59,249
You'll be getting a subpoena.

377
00:23:28,474 --> 00:23:30,218
Cassie, is that you?

378
00:23:30,242 --> 00:23:32,120
Hey, whoa, whoa!

379
00:23:32,144 --> 00:23:33,688
Put that down, okay?

380
00:23:33,712 --> 00:23:34,990
You got me.

381
00:23:35,014 --> 00:23:36,825
Who are you?

382
00:23:36,849 --> 00:23:39,561
Judy Benson.

383
00:23:39,585 --> 00:23:42,063
Benson?

384
00:23:42,087 --> 00:23:44,165
That's right, hon.

385
00:23:44,189 --> 00:23:45,667
Kevin's other wife.

386
00:24:06,813 --> 00:24:08,913
Where is she? Kitchen.

387
00:24:22,661 --> 00:24:25,073
Well.

388
00:24:25,097 --> 00:24:28,276
Ben Matlock, Judy Benson.

389
00:24:28,300 --> 00:24:29,544
Wife Number 3.

390
00:24:29,568 --> 00:24:31,880
How do you do? Hello.

391
00:24:31,904 --> 00:24:33,515
Coffee, Ben?

392
00:24:33,539 --> 00:24:35,939
Oh, no. I'd better not.

393
00:24:37,576 --> 00:24:40,522
How...? How long have
you been married to Kevin?

394
00:24:40,546 --> 00:24:42,123
Uh, three years.

395
00:24:43,482 --> 00:24:45,482
Guess that makes
me the senior member.

396
00:24:47,653 --> 00:24:48,931
Judy is a showgirl

397
00:24:48,955 --> 00:24:51,021
at a casino in Atlantic City.

398
00:24:52,424 --> 00:24:53,457
Oh.

399
00:24:54,793 --> 00:24:58,240
Uh, that's where
she and Kevin lived.

400
00:24:58,264 --> 00:24:59,841
Atlantic City?

401
00:24:59,865 --> 00:25:01,943
Why is everybody so surprised?

402
00:25:01,967 --> 00:25:03,911
Atlantic City was
his kind of town.

403
00:25:03,935 --> 00:25:06,381
He loved it. He thrived on it.

404
00:25:06,405 --> 00:25:08,382
You know, the shows,

405
00:25:08,406 --> 00:25:13,087
the gambling, the
money, the action.

406
00:25:13,111 --> 00:25:16,825
You mean Kevin wasn't
in bed by 10 every night?

407
00:25:16,849 --> 00:25:20,284
Honey, he'd be lucky if he
got in bed by 10 in the morning.

408
00:25:22,888 --> 00:25:24,833
He'd come home
from one of his trips

409
00:25:24,857 --> 00:25:28,403
and we'd celebrate for days.

410
00:25:33,632 --> 00:25:36,599
Hi, doll. How'd
it go at the track?

411
00:25:45,644 --> 00:25:47,856
All I could think about
was coming home.

412
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:49,313
Oh. Hm.

413
00:25:58,089 --> 00:26:00,535
He was fun when he won.

414
00:26:00,559 --> 00:26:03,472
He was even fun when he lost.

415
00:26:03,496 --> 00:26:05,976
He was perfect
for Atlantic City.

416
00:26:07,466 --> 00:26:09,511
He was perfect for me.

417
00:26:09,535 --> 00:26:13,949
So... for three years,

418
00:26:13,973 --> 00:26:16,617
you thought Kevin was, uh,

419
00:26:16,641 --> 00:26:19,154
just a businessman
who traveled a lot?

420
00:26:19,178 --> 00:26:20,188
Yeah.

421
00:26:20,212 --> 00:26:22,457
But then I started
to miss things.

422
00:26:22,481 --> 00:26:23,825
You know, little
things at first,

423
00:26:23,849 --> 00:26:26,361
like, uh, jewelry.

424
00:26:26,385 --> 00:26:29,397
Then I discovered
$30,000 had vanished

425
00:26:29,421 --> 00:26:31,899
from my private savings account.

426
00:26:31,923 --> 00:26:33,968
Yeah? Yeah, last week.

427
00:26:33,992 --> 00:26:36,171
So I hired a private detective,

428
00:26:36,195 --> 00:26:39,941
discovered he used the
money to buy a house.

429
00:26:39,965 --> 00:26:41,476
This house.

430
00:26:41,500 --> 00:26:43,812
I don't believe this.

431
00:26:43,836 --> 00:26:45,279
So I flew down right away

432
00:26:45,303 --> 00:26:48,583
to find out once and for
all what was going on.

433
00:26:48,607 --> 00:26:50,185
But I was too late.

434
00:26:50,209 --> 00:26:53,588
I got in yesterday and read
that he'd been murdered.

435
00:26:53,612 --> 00:26:56,058
And that if I wanted
to claim to be his wife,

436
00:26:56,082 --> 00:26:57,993
I had to stand in line.

437
00:26:58,017 --> 00:27:01,530
Well, I decided it'd be
best not to get involved.

438
00:27:01,554 --> 00:27:04,499
Why did you break
in here tonight?

439
00:27:04,523 --> 00:27:06,768
Well, the last thing
Kevin took from me

440
00:27:06,792 --> 00:27:08,804
was a watch he'd given
me from Christmas.

441
00:27:08,828 --> 00:27:11,707
Uh, it had my
name engraved on it.

442
00:27:11,731 --> 00:27:14,242
Well, I was afraid
some smart cop

443
00:27:14,266 --> 00:27:16,311
might trace it back to me.

444
00:27:16,335 --> 00:27:18,480
So I figured if he
hadn't hocked it yet,

445
00:27:18,504 --> 00:27:20,749
it'd be in his briefcase.

446
00:27:20,773 --> 00:27:24,252
Kevin had $300 in cash
on him when he died.

447
00:27:24,276 --> 00:27:25,586
That's it, then.

448
00:27:25,610 --> 00:27:28,690
Kevin was really something
of a rat, wasn't he?

449
00:27:28,714 --> 00:27:35,018
Maybe Kevin loved
not wisely, but too much.

450
00:27:37,956 --> 00:27:41,191
I think that's a very nice
way to remember him.

451
00:28:00,646 --> 00:28:02,179
Hi, Ben.

452
00:28:06,318 --> 00:28:09,297
Benjamin.

453
00:28:09,321 --> 00:28:11,121
It wouldn't come out.

454
00:28:12,424 --> 00:28:13,969
Oh. I knew it wouldn't.

455
00:28:13,993 --> 00:28:16,160
Spaghetti sauce never does.

456
00:28:17,830 --> 00:28:20,274
Alfredo. Before he
sold to that chain,

457
00:28:20,298 --> 00:28:22,644
they used to bring a big
napkin, put it all around,

458
00:28:22,668 --> 00:28:25,380
never used to
get my tie in stuff.

459
00:28:25,404 --> 00:28:28,049
But you liked the
spaghetti, didn't you?

460
00:28:28,073 --> 00:28:30,352
Oh, it was okay.

461
00:28:30,376 --> 00:28:31,987
Kind of stayed with
me. Garlic, I guess.

462
00:28:32,011 --> 00:28:33,788
How about you? Oh, fine.

463
00:28:33,812 --> 00:28:36,091
Well, you liked the music. Yeah.

464
00:28:36,115 --> 00:28:37,893
It was okay as accordions go.

465
00:28:42,021 --> 00:28:43,865
Where's your client?

466
00:28:43,889 --> 00:28:46,601
Over there, talking
to Mrs. Benson.

467
00:28:49,595 --> 00:28:52,307
Call that Mrs. Bensons.

468
00:28:52,331 --> 00:28:56,311
You know... You
know, that guy...

469
00:28:56,335 --> 00:28:57,846
I know it's wrong.

470
00:28:57,870 --> 00:29:01,683
I-I know it's wrong,
but you gotta...

471
00:29:01,707 --> 00:29:03,418
You gotta admire...

472
00:29:03,442 --> 00:29:06,454
No, not admire, not admire.

473
00:29:06,478 --> 00:29:10,558
Wonder. You know, three.

474
00:29:10,582 --> 00:29:12,527
You're not a
chauvinist, are you?

475
00:29:12,551 --> 00:29:13,828
Oh, no, no, no.

476
00:29:13,852 --> 00:29:17,265
I just wonder why
he married all of them.

477
00:29:17,289 --> 00:29:20,590
Call it a weakness
in his character.

478
00:29:21,927 --> 00:29:25,862
Well, this case
shouldn't take too long.

479
00:29:33,672 --> 00:29:36,584
Good news. I think I
got this case wrapped up.

480
00:29:36,608 --> 00:29:37,919
I'm glad to hear it

481
00:29:37,943 --> 00:29:40,922
because that's just what
the district attorney thinks.

482
00:29:40,946 --> 00:29:42,123
And in response

483
00:29:42,147 --> 00:29:44,725
to the decedent's
brother's accusation,

484
00:29:44,749 --> 00:29:46,527
did the defendant say anything?

485
00:29:46,551 --> 00:29:47,795
Yes.

486
00:29:47,819 --> 00:29:49,264
"He was alive when I left.

487
00:29:49,288 --> 00:29:51,632
"We struggled. I guess
I hit him with that vase,

488
00:29:51,656 --> 00:29:53,334
but he was alive."

489
00:29:53,358 --> 00:29:56,638
This is marked People's
Exhibit Number 8.

490
00:29:56,662 --> 00:29:59,007
Do you recognize it?
Yes. That's the vase

491
00:29:59,031 --> 00:30:00,142
that we found next to the body

492
00:30:00,166 --> 00:30:01,910
and which was
stained with blood.

493
00:30:01,934 --> 00:30:04,512
It has also been stipulated,

494
00:30:04,536 --> 00:30:06,181
by the defense,

495
00:30:06,205 --> 00:30:09,084
that the fingerprints
found on this vase

496
00:30:09,108 --> 00:30:12,754
matched those
belonging to the defendant.

497
00:30:12,778 --> 00:30:14,577
Thank you, Lieutenant Herrmann.

498
00:30:15,981 --> 00:30:17,658
It was amusing.

499
00:30:17,682 --> 00:30:20,361
I was rather delighted
at the whole scene.

500
00:30:20,385 --> 00:30:23,631
What about Diane Benson?

501
00:30:23,655 --> 00:30:27,035
Mm, she seemed
surprised. Shocked, really.

502
00:30:27,059 --> 00:30:30,004
Surprised? Shocked?

503
00:30:30,028 --> 00:30:31,205
Yes.

504
00:30:31,229 --> 00:30:33,141
What did she do?

505
00:30:33,165 --> 00:30:36,277
She blew up, flew
out of the restaurant.

506
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:38,046
Oh, she was angry?

507
00:30:39,738 --> 00:30:41,549
If looks could kill.

508
00:30:41,573 --> 00:30:43,885
Objection. Non-responsive.

509
00:30:43,909 --> 00:30:47,322
Sustained. Was she angry or not?

510
00:30:47,346 --> 00:30:49,858
She was madder than hell.

511
00:30:49,882 --> 00:30:52,894
Now, Mrs. Berino,

512
00:30:52,918 --> 00:30:56,898
you are certain that
the person you saw

513
00:30:56,922 --> 00:31:00,535
entering their house that night
was the defendant, Diane Benson?

514
00:31:00,559 --> 00:31:01,669
Oh, yes.

515
00:31:01,693 --> 00:31:03,137
I can see their front door

516
00:31:03,161 --> 00:31:04,839
clearly through my window.

517
00:31:04,863 --> 00:31:06,774
First, Mrs. Benson came home.

518
00:31:06,798 --> 00:31:08,843
Then Mr. Benson came home.

519
00:31:08,867 --> 00:31:10,979
And then Mrs. Benson left.

520
00:31:11,003 --> 00:31:13,881
Do you recall
what time she left?

521
00:31:13,905 --> 00:31:15,783
Between 9 and 10.

522
00:31:15,807 --> 00:31:19,387
I know because of the
TV show that was on.

523
00:31:19,411 --> 00:31:21,555
Did you see anybody else

524
00:31:21,579 --> 00:31:24,492
enter or leave the
house that night?

525
00:31:24,516 --> 00:31:25,826
No one.

526
00:31:25,850 --> 00:31:27,762
Are you positive?

527
00:31:27,786 --> 00:31:29,897
I'm positive.

528
00:31:29,921 --> 00:31:31,633
Thank you, Mrs. Berino.

529
00:31:31,657 --> 00:31:33,434
No further questions.

530
00:31:33,458 --> 00:31:35,837
Cross-examine?

531
00:31:35,861 --> 00:31:39,007
No questions. Reserve the
right to recall this witness.

532
00:31:39,031 --> 00:31:40,875
You may step down.

533
00:31:40,899 --> 00:31:43,044
The prosecution
rests, Your Honor.

534
00:31:43,068 --> 00:31:45,913
Very well. Um, Miss Thomas,

535
00:31:45,937 --> 00:31:47,414
is the defense ready?

536
00:31:47,438 --> 00:31:48,749
We are, Your Honor.

537
00:31:48,773 --> 00:31:51,753
For our first witness, the
defense calls Judy Benson.

538
00:31:51,777 --> 00:31:53,888
And you too, were
married to Kevin Benson

539
00:31:53,912 --> 00:31:55,657
at the time of his death?

540
00:31:55,681 --> 00:31:57,358
Yes.

541
00:31:57,382 --> 00:32:00,795
Isn't it true that Kevin Benson
helped himself to $30,000

542
00:32:00,819 --> 00:32:02,630
of your money without
your knowledge?

543
00:32:02,654 --> 00:32:05,399
Yeah.

544
00:32:05,423 --> 00:32:08,102
Thirty-thousand dollars
that he used to buy

545
00:32:08,126 --> 00:32:11,839
a house here in Atlanta for
himself and another woman.

546
00:32:11,863 --> 00:32:15,043
In fact, another wife.

547
00:32:15,067 --> 00:32:16,477
Yes.

548
00:32:16,501 --> 00:32:19,380
It must have come as quite
a shock when you found out.

549
00:32:19,404 --> 00:32:21,949
Well, I didn't know
about the other wives

550
00:32:21,973 --> 00:32:24,118
until after he was dead.

551
00:32:24,142 --> 00:32:25,719
I believe you did arrive in town

552
00:32:25,743 --> 00:32:28,278
the day before he was
murdered, didn't you?

553
00:32:29,948 --> 00:32:31,493
So, what if I did?

554
00:32:31,517 --> 00:32:33,728
Well, didn't you in fact
go to Kevin Benson's home

555
00:32:33,752 --> 00:32:34,929
the night of the murder?

556
00:32:34,953 --> 00:32:37,732
No.

557
00:32:37,756 --> 00:32:41,703
Mrs. Benson, at the
request of any resident,

558
00:32:41,727 --> 00:32:44,138
the Atlanta Police will
run a computer check

559
00:32:44,162 --> 00:32:47,074
of all automobiles
reported to be parked

560
00:32:47,098 --> 00:32:49,477
or prowling in residential
neighborhoods.

561
00:32:49,501 --> 00:32:52,146
All right. I-I was in town early

562
00:32:52,170 --> 00:32:56,050
and, uh, I knew
about his other wife.

563
00:32:56,074 --> 00:32:58,018
I planned to confront
him, but I didn't.

564
00:32:58,042 --> 00:33:01,222
I, uh... I just drove
around the block,

565
00:33:01,246 --> 00:33:03,958
and, uh, then I parked

566
00:33:03,982 --> 00:33:06,828
and tried to get
up enough nerve.

567
00:33:06,852 --> 00:33:08,997
Then what?

568
00:33:09,021 --> 00:33:10,298
Well, you've gotta realize

569
00:33:10,322 --> 00:33:12,567
this whole thing
is kind of spooky.

570
00:33:12,591 --> 00:33:16,103
Mrs. Benson, what happened?

571
00:33:16,127 --> 00:33:19,774
Well, I'm walking up this
alley when all of a sudden this...

572
00:33:19,798 --> 00:33:21,142
Yes?

573
00:33:21,166 --> 00:33:24,345
This person comes

574
00:33:24,369 --> 00:33:26,614
rising out of the dumpster.

575
00:33:26,638 --> 00:33:28,616
Scared the hell out of me,

576
00:33:28,640 --> 00:33:31,452
so I ran straight to my
car, started the engine,

577
00:33:31,476 --> 00:33:34,155
and didn't take my foot
off the gas until I was...

578
00:33:34,179 --> 00:33:36,924
I-I don't know
how far away I was.

579
00:33:36,948 --> 00:33:38,793
Oh, I see.

580
00:33:38,817 --> 00:33:41,830
Some person crawling
around in a dumpster

581
00:33:41,854 --> 00:33:44,432
in the middle of the
night suddenly appeared,

582
00:33:44,456 --> 00:33:47,569
scared you, and then you left.

583
00:33:47,593 --> 00:33:49,737
Yeah.

584
00:33:49,761 --> 00:33:51,139
Well, I suppose it's too bad

585
00:33:51,163 --> 00:33:54,976
this mysterious figure
can't corroborate your story.

586
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,266
Oh, she can.

587
00:33:59,037 --> 00:34:00,904
It was her.

588
00:34:11,483 --> 00:34:15,663
I was looking for
scraps for my cats.

589
00:34:15,687 --> 00:34:17,565
I have so many.

590
00:34:17,589 --> 00:34:20,624
I can barely afford
to feed myself.

591
00:34:22,127 --> 00:34:24,661
I didn't want the
neighbors to know.

592
00:34:27,599 --> 00:34:30,445
What happened when
Mrs. Benson saw you?

593
00:34:30,469 --> 00:34:33,481
She jumped about a
foot in the air and she ran

594
00:34:33,505 --> 00:34:37,485
to her car and drove
away just like she said.

595
00:34:37,509 --> 00:34:39,220
Then what happened?

596
00:34:39,244 --> 00:34:41,456
I went back to my house.

597
00:34:41,480 --> 00:34:42,790
Well, then, it's true

598
00:34:42,814 --> 00:34:45,326
you weren't watching the
Benson house all evening.

599
00:34:45,350 --> 00:34:48,429
And someone could've entered
the home, killed Kevin Benson,

600
00:34:48,453 --> 00:34:50,765
and left without you
seeing him or her.

601
00:34:50,789 --> 00:34:53,767
Objection. Calls
for speculation.

602
00:34:53,791 --> 00:34:54,968
Overruled.

603
00:34:54,992 --> 00:34:56,759
Answer the question.

604
00:34:58,396 --> 00:35:00,808
I suppose so.

605
00:35:00,832 --> 00:35:03,077
Yes.

606
00:35:03,101 --> 00:35:06,147
Mrs. Berino, why did you lie?

607
00:35:06,171 --> 00:35:08,950
I had to lie.

608
00:35:08,974 --> 00:35:12,420
I couldn't tell the police
what I was really doing.

609
00:35:12,444 --> 00:35:16,491
They might've reported
me to some animal group.

610
00:35:16,515 --> 00:35:21,195
Maybe even put me in some home.

611
00:35:21,219 --> 00:35:24,487
I had to lie.

612
00:35:26,391 --> 00:35:28,124
No further questions.

613
00:35:34,532 --> 00:35:37,545
I thought I had Judy
Benson. I really did.

614
00:35:37,569 --> 00:35:40,381
Well, it happens.

615
00:35:40,405 --> 00:35:42,049
I've been in fixes like that

616
00:35:42,073 --> 00:35:44,619
where I thought I had him

617
00:35:44,643 --> 00:35:47,755
and I didn't have him,

618
00:35:47,779 --> 00:35:50,012
if it's any consolation.

619
00:35:50,816 --> 00:35:52,816
It's no consolation.

620
00:35:54,419 --> 00:35:55,985
It was just a thought.

621
00:36:21,180 --> 00:36:22,590
Hi.

622
00:36:22,614 --> 00:36:23,858
Mr. Matlock, hi.

623
00:36:23,882 --> 00:36:27,395
Hi. I knocked. I guess
you didn't hear me.

624
00:36:27,419 --> 00:36:28,896
Packing, huh?

625
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:32,867
I figured the house will
sell better with me out of it.

626
00:36:32,891 --> 00:36:35,102
I feel terrible about
Kevin's using Judy's money

627
00:36:35,126 --> 00:36:36,871
like that to buy it.

628
00:36:36,895 --> 00:36:38,906
I-I want to pay her back.

629
00:36:38,930 --> 00:36:40,975
Well, remember now,

630
00:36:40,999 --> 00:36:43,233
she can still be the killer.

631
00:36:44,302 --> 00:36:45,613
No, she's not.

632
00:36:45,637 --> 00:36:47,448
What makes you so sure?

633
00:36:47,472 --> 00:36:49,484
Because I told her I wasn't.

634
00:36:49,508 --> 00:36:51,085
And I believe her.

635
00:36:51,109 --> 00:36:52,853
You want a cup of coffee?

636
00:36:52,877 --> 00:36:55,323
I haven't packed the mugs
and the coffee's already made.

637
00:36:55,347 --> 00:36:56,924
Oh, no. No, don't
go to any bother.

638
00:36:56,948 --> 00:36:59,126
I just stopped by to
see if you know anything

639
00:36:59,150 --> 00:37:05,633
about, uh, Kevin's
other wife, uh, Liz.

640
00:37:05,657 --> 00:37:09,170
Don't forget all those
things you have in the buffet.

641
00:37:09,194 --> 00:37:10,438
Hello.

642
00:37:10,462 --> 00:37:12,206
Hi.

643
00:37:12,230 --> 00:37:15,643
I was about to ask
where you were or are.

644
00:37:15,667 --> 00:37:17,512
Well, now you know.

645
00:37:17,536 --> 00:37:19,780
I'm a member of this sorority.

646
00:37:24,609 --> 00:37:26,854
Maybe I will have
that cup of coffee.

647
00:37:26,878 --> 00:37:28,556
Uh, no, no, Mr. Matlock.

648
00:37:28,580 --> 00:37:30,725
Briefcases do not
go on the table.

649
00:37:30,749 --> 00:37:32,126
Really? They don't?

650
00:37:32,150 --> 00:37:34,161
Not in the Benson household.

651
00:37:34,185 --> 00:37:35,329
Oh, where do they go?

652
00:37:35,353 --> 00:37:37,665
On the floor right
next to the front door.

653
00:37:37,689 --> 00:37:40,768
So your wife's sure to trip
over it at least once a week.

654
00:37:43,561 --> 00:37:45,840
Right over there.

655
00:37:45,864 --> 00:37:48,075
Okay.

656
00:37:48,099 --> 00:37:51,545
Yeah. If nothing else, Kevin
was a creature of habit, wasn't he?

657
00:37:51,569 --> 00:37:54,349
You know, I think one of
the most annoying things

658
00:37:54,373 --> 00:37:55,950
about him was the
way he made me strain

659
00:37:55,974 --> 00:37:57,818
the seeds out of
his orange juice.

660
00:37:59,311 --> 00:38:01,655
I was always
afraid I'd forget that.

661
00:38:14,226 --> 00:38:16,204
Mr. Matlock?

662
00:38:16,228 --> 00:38:18,072
Your Honor, at this time,
the defense would like

663
00:38:18,096 --> 00:38:20,174
to recall Clark
Benson to the stand.

664
00:38:20,198 --> 00:38:21,442
Defendant's co-counsel,

665
00:38:21,466 --> 00:38:24,066
Benjamin Matlock,
will be examining him.

666
00:38:26,070 --> 00:38:28,316
Go get him.

667
00:38:28,340 --> 00:38:29,806
You betcha.

668
00:38:33,778 --> 00:38:36,946
Kevin Benson was, uh...

669
00:38:38,817 --> 00:38:41,161
Was your younger
brother, is that correct?

670
00:38:41,185 --> 00:38:42,463
Yes.

671
00:38:42,487 --> 00:38:44,887
Were you close as
you were growing up?

672
00:38:45,556 --> 00:38:46,934
Yes.

673
00:38:46,958 --> 00:38:48,468
Did you approve of him?

674
00:38:48,492 --> 00:38:50,171
Well, of course.

675
00:38:50,195 --> 00:38:53,474
Kevin was outstanding
in whatever he did.

676
00:38:53,498 --> 00:38:54,575
Did, uh...?

677
00:38:57,836 --> 00:39:00,314
Did he have a
lot of girlfriends?

678
00:39:00,338 --> 00:39:03,384
I suppose the usual.

679
00:39:03,408 --> 00:39:05,286
How about yourself?

680
00:39:05,310 --> 00:39:07,888
Oh, the usual.

681
00:39:07,912 --> 00:39:11,881
The usual, or not many?

682
00:39:16,421 --> 00:39:18,265
Uh, are you a married man?

683
00:39:18,289 --> 00:39:19,533
No.

684
00:39:19,557 --> 00:39:23,537
Is there a relationship
at the present time?

685
00:39:23,561 --> 00:39:25,473
No. Ahem.

686
00:39:25,497 --> 00:39:27,608
How about in the past?

687
00:39:27,632 --> 00:39:30,934
The usual, or not many?

688
00:39:33,705 --> 00:39:40,154
Mr. Benson, we know
that... That Kevin had,

689
00:39:40,178 --> 00:39:43,724
uh, three wives.

690
00:39:43,748 --> 00:39:46,916
Now, any way you look at it,

691
00:39:48,520 --> 00:39:52,500
that has to be
trying on a person.

692
00:39:52,524 --> 00:39:55,736
In many ways,
including financially,

693
00:39:55,760 --> 00:39:57,071
wouldn't you say?

694
00:39:57,095 --> 00:39:59,139
I... Yeah, I suppose.

695
00:39:59,163 --> 00:40:04,077
But Kevin was a
successful businessman.

696
00:40:04,101 --> 00:40:06,714
Yes. Quite.

697
00:40:06,738 --> 00:40:08,883
You know, that... That's what

698
00:40:08,907 --> 00:40:14,121
is interesting to
me, along with, uh,

699
00:40:14,145 --> 00:40:19,193
everything else,
is that in spite

700
00:40:19,217 --> 00:40:21,094
of how bright Kevin was,

701
00:40:21,118 --> 00:40:23,664
and how successful
he seemed to be,

702
00:40:23,688 --> 00:40:26,734
he didn't actually own

703
00:40:26,758 --> 00:40:28,836
anything business-wise, did he?

704
00:40:28,860 --> 00:40:31,839
I mean, he just... He
just worked for you.

705
00:40:31,863 --> 00:40:35,609
Kevin was a genius
at what he did.

706
00:40:35,633 --> 00:40:38,979
It made for a great partnership.

707
00:40:39,003 --> 00:40:42,183
But that's not
really the case, is it?

708
00:40:42,207 --> 00:40:45,886
He wasn't literally
your partner.

709
00:40:45,910 --> 00:40:48,355
Technically, the
company was in my name.

710
00:40:48,379 --> 00:40:49,824
You started it, didn't you?

711
00:40:49,848 --> 00:40:51,959
Yes. And you owned it?

712
00:40:51,983 --> 00:40:55,896
Yes. But it was a partnership
in the true sense of the word.

713
00:40:55,920 --> 00:40:58,933
In every sense of the
word except financially.

714
00:40:58,957 --> 00:41:00,267
Kevin worked for you.

715
00:41:00,291 --> 00:41:03,304
He was literally an employee.

716
00:41:03,328 --> 00:41:06,507
I suppose if you want to
be legalistic about it, he was.

717
00:41:06,531 --> 00:41:09,277
But neither of us ever
thought about it that way.

718
00:41:09,301 --> 00:41:11,745
You didn't think
about it that way

719
00:41:11,769 --> 00:41:14,015
even after you discovered

720
00:41:14,039 --> 00:41:15,916
he had been dipping in the till?

721
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:18,886
Who said he was
dipping into the till?

722
00:41:18,910 --> 00:41:21,022
Didn't you order an audit

723
00:41:21,046 --> 00:41:24,124
of your company's
finances two months ago?

724
00:41:24,148 --> 00:41:25,225
Yes.

725
00:41:25,249 --> 00:41:27,361
And didn't the auditor discover

726
00:41:27,385 --> 00:41:30,697
certain discrepancies

727
00:41:30,721 --> 00:41:33,501
in Kevin's withdrawal
of company funds?

728
00:41:33,525 --> 00:41:34,669
Kevin and I discussed it

729
00:41:34,693 --> 00:41:37,872
and we worked out a
schedule for repayment.

730
00:41:37,896 --> 00:41:39,841
Naturally, I was disappointed
this had occurred,

731
00:41:39,865 --> 00:41:42,632
but he swore it would
never happen again.

732
00:41:45,370 --> 00:41:49,249
I think the night of the murder,

733
00:41:49,273 --> 00:41:54,288
you knew about at
least two of his wives,

734
00:41:54,312 --> 00:41:56,457
and you went over to
confront your brother

735
00:41:56,481 --> 00:41:58,725
on that and the audit.

736
00:41:58,749 --> 00:42:00,294
That's not true.

737
00:42:00,318 --> 00:42:02,129
You didn't go into his house

738
00:42:02,153 --> 00:42:04,398
after Diane Benson
left that night?

739
00:42:04,422 --> 00:42:06,099
No.

740
00:42:06,123 --> 00:42:08,135
And you didn't argue with him,

741
00:42:08,159 --> 00:42:10,137
and during some
kind of struggle,

742
00:42:10,161 --> 00:42:12,540
he fell and hit his
head and was killed?

743
00:42:12,564 --> 00:42:14,363
Absolutely not.

744
00:42:15,567 --> 00:42:17,278
Your Honor,

745
00:42:17,302 --> 00:42:21,182
I'd like to enter
into evidence...

746
00:42:21,206 --> 00:42:22,349
this briefcase.

747
00:42:22,373 --> 00:42:27,788
Call it, uh, uh,
Defense Exhibit A.

748
00:42:27,812 --> 00:42:29,856
You recognize this briefcase?

749
00:42:29,880 --> 00:42:31,725
Yes. That's my
brother's briefcase.

750
00:42:31,749 --> 00:42:35,329
And it has his initials.
KHB, Kevin Henry Benson.

751
00:42:35,353 --> 00:42:37,531
Yes. Do you have
a briefcase like it?

752
00:42:37,555 --> 00:42:40,334
Yes. They're identical except
for the initials, of course.

753
00:42:40,358 --> 00:42:42,636
And your initials are?

754
00:42:42,660 --> 00:42:45,428
CLB, Clark Lee Benson.

755
00:42:46,764 --> 00:42:52,613
Uh, Your Honor, ah,

756
00:42:52,637 --> 00:42:56,572
this is People's Exhibit 15.

757
00:42:58,476 --> 00:43:01,254
It's a police lab photograph

758
00:43:01,278 --> 00:43:05,025
of the scene of the crime.

759
00:43:05,049 --> 00:43:10,564
Let's call this
Defense Exhibit B.

760
00:43:10,588 --> 00:43:17,338
This is a detailed
blowup of an object

761
00:43:17,362 --> 00:43:21,275
in the back of the
police lab's photograph.

762
00:43:21,299 --> 00:43:24,211
What is that, Mr. Benson?

763
00:43:24,235 --> 00:43:26,335
That's Kevin's briefcase.

764
00:43:27,205 --> 00:43:30,907
And it has his initials, KHB.

765
00:43:34,012 --> 00:43:36,557
Are my eyes deceiving me,

766
00:43:36,581 --> 00:43:41,250
or do those initials read CLB?

767
00:43:44,322 --> 00:43:46,166
Those are your initials.

768
00:43:46,190 --> 00:43:48,869
That isn't Kevin's briefcase.

769
00:43:48,893 --> 00:43:51,072
It's yours.

770
00:43:51,096 --> 00:43:54,041
Yes, well, we must've, uh,

771
00:43:54,065 --> 00:43:57,311
uh, gotten them mixed
up at the office that day.

772
00:43:57,335 --> 00:44:00,781
If that were the case,

773
00:44:00,805 --> 00:44:03,350
why do you suppose if Kevin

774
00:44:03,374 --> 00:44:05,786
brought your briefcase

775
00:44:05,810 --> 00:44:10,591
to his house by mistake,
why do you suppose

776
00:44:10,615 --> 00:44:12,515
he'd put it on the sofa,

777
00:44:13,751 --> 00:44:16,930
instead of on the floor

778
00:44:16,954 --> 00:44:20,455
by the front door,
as he always did?

779
00:44:26,097 --> 00:44:29,543
I bet you switched
those briefcases

780
00:44:29,567 --> 00:44:31,311
the next morning.

781
00:44:31,335 --> 00:44:37,318
Sometime when the
police weren't looking,

782
00:44:37,342 --> 00:44:41,188
you picked up your
briefcase from the sofa

783
00:44:41,212 --> 00:44:44,324
and put Kevin's
there in its place.

784
00:44:44,348 --> 00:44:45,793
These briefcases

785
00:44:45,817 --> 00:44:48,629
didn't get mixed
up at the office.

786
00:44:48,653 --> 00:44:55,335
They got mixed up at
his house, that night,

787
00:44:55,359 --> 00:44:58,794
just after you murdered
him, didn't they?

788
00:45:01,099 --> 00:45:03,210
He was a liar.

789
00:45:05,503 --> 00:45:07,703
He was a cheat.

790
00:45:08,606 --> 00:45:11,166
He was always a womanizer.

791
00:45:14,011 --> 00:45:20,016
But he was my
brother, so I took him in.

792
00:45:24,889 --> 00:45:27,467
Three wives.

793
00:45:27,491 --> 00:45:31,671
He cheated them, he cheated me.

794
00:45:31,695 --> 00:45:38,434
All my life, work
is all I've ever had.

795
00:45:41,772 --> 00:45:45,085
And he took that too.

796
00:45:45,109 --> 00:45:47,221
I'm sorry, Mr. Benson.

797
00:45:47,245 --> 00:45:51,158
Just so everyone understands,

798
00:45:51,182 --> 00:45:52,659
during your struggle with him,

799
00:45:52,683 --> 00:45:57,397
you shoved him, he hit his head,

800
00:45:57,421 --> 00:45:59,933
he fell to the floor dead.

801
00:45:59,957 --> 00:46:03,103
Then you panicked

802
00:46:03,127 --> 00:46:05,705
and instead of picking
up your briefcase,

803
00:46:05,729 --> 00:46:07,341
you picked up his briefcase

804
00:46:07,365 --> 00:46:10,010
by the door and ran.

805
00:46:10,034 --> 00:46:13,936
The next day, you switched them.

806
00:46:16,040 --> 00:46:17,573
That about it?

807
00:46:20,111 --> 00:46:21,677
Yes.

808
00:46:26,484 --> 00:46:27,694
Well, I guess we did it.

809
00:46:27,718 --> 00:46:29,596
Sure did.

810
00:46:29,620 --> 00:46:32,166
It really was something.

811
00:46:32,190 --> 00:46:33,455
Hey, Julie?

812
00:46:35,793 --> 00:46:39,773
You know, I still
can't get over that guy.

813
00:46:39,797 --> 00:46:44,900
I mean, three.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
